 \section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}
 \textbf{Leakage Testing:}\label{sec:validating-speculative-execution-defenses}
-Prior work validates speculative-execution security at three stages of a design: it tests fabricated CPUs, verifies or fuzzes RTL, and, before RTL, model-checks hand-written models of early-stage designs~\cite{pensieve} or tests simulated Spectre defenses. Revizor~\cite{revizor} and its follow-ups~\cite{oleksenko2023hideseekspectresefficient,hofmann2023speculationatfault} fuzz commercial CPUs against hardware-software contracts (\S\ref{sec:bg-contracts}), and Trevex~\cite{weber2026trevex} detects data-flow transient-execution vulnerabilities. In RTL, tools search for transient-execution vulnerabilities through differential execution~\cite{hur2022specdoctor,xu2025dejavuzz}, gadget-driven simulation~\cite{ghaniyoun2021introspectre}, information-flow tracking~\cite{rostami2024specure}, or contract coverage~\cite{geier2025coverage}. Scam-V~\cite{nemati2020scamv} validates side-channel models against hardware, and software tools test programs for Spectre gadgets~\cite{oleksenko2020specfuzz} or against leakage models~\cite{barthe2024lmtest}. In gem5, SpecCheck~\cite{mckevitt2023speccheck} identifies vulnerable transient execution in a running program.
