 The closest prior work is AMuLeT~\cite{amulet} (\S\S\ref{sec:known-bugs} and~\ref{sec:comparison-prior-work}), which fuzzes simulated Spectre defenses against the hardware-software contracts \contract{CT-SEQ} and \contract{ARCH-SEQ}~\cite[\S 3.1]{amulet}; four of its nine reports fall outside the defenses' security guarantees. Protean's fuzzer extends AMuLeT with an adversary model that observes the cycle at which each instruction reaches each pipeline stage~\cite[\S VII-B1]{protean}. AMuLeT varies mispredictions by keeping predictor state across test cases~\cite[\S 3.2]{amulet} and root-causes each contract violation manually, which took hours to days per violation~\cite[\S 3.3]{amulet}. In contrast, \toolname{} checks one run against the defense's security guarantee, forces each test's mispredictions (\S\ref{sec:speculation-directives}), and synthesizes each test from an execution graph that identifies the transmitter, the protected data, and the speculation primitive responsible (\S\ref{sec:leakage-predicate}), so only locating the responsible code in the defense is manual.
 
