 Beyond the generators of \S\ref{sec:bg-axiomatic}, litmus tests have been derived from cycles of relaxed orderings~\cite{alglave2010fences}, generated to contrast memory models~\cite{maradhaim2011litmus}, run on hardware~\cite{alglave2011litmus,perple}, and mutated to evaluate test suites~\cite{mcmutants}. The Check tools verify memory consistency model implementations, from pipelines to RTL, by analyzing axiomatic models of microarchitectures, for a suite of litmus tests or, with PipeProof, for all programs~\cite{lustig2014pipecheck,manerkar2015ccicheck,lustig2016coatcheck,trippel2017tricheck,manerkar2017rtlcheck,manerkar2018pipeproof,manerkar2020realitycheck}; memory models~\cite{memsynth} and microarchitectural axioms~\cite{norman2023pipesynth,hsiao2021rtl2uspec} have also been synthesized. Isla~\cite{armstrong2021isla} evaluates litmus tests against full ISA semantics~\cite{armstrong2019sail}, and TransForm~\cite{hossain2020transform} synthesizes litmus tests from axiomatic transistency models. \toolname{} synthesizes security litmus tests from a defense's specification.
 
 \noindent\textbf{Spectre Defenses:}\label{sec:spectre-hardware-defenses}
-Spectre defenses vary in the side channels and kinds of speculation they address and in the software changes they require~\cite{hu2021sok}, \cite[\S X]{protean}. Beyond those in \S\ref{sec:introduction}, some address only cache-based channels~\cite{taram2019csf,li2019conditional,kiriansky2018dawg} or only some speculation primitives~\cite{koruyeh2020speccfi}; others rely on source-level secrecy annotations~\cite{yu2019oisa,daniel2023prospect,schwarz2020context,fustos2019spectreguard} or require programmers to change memory layout~\cite{green2023safebet,kim2024perspective}; and security-preserving optimizations speed up existing defenses~\cite{tran2020clearing,zhao2020invarspec,hajiabadi2024levioso}.
+Spectre defenses vary in the side channels and kinds of speculation they address and in the software changes they require~\cite{hu2021sok}, \cite[\S X]{protean}. Beyond those in \S\ref{sec:introduction}, some address only cache-based channels~\cite{taram2019csf,li2019conditional,kiriansky2018dawg} or only some speculation primitives~\cite{koruyeh2020speccfi}; others rely on source-level secrecy annotations~\cite{yu2019oisa,daniel2023prospect,schwarz2020context,fustos2019spectreguard} \cutrep{or require programmers to change memory layout~\cite{green2023safebet,kim2024perspective}}{or require memory-layout changes~\cite{green2023safebet,kim2024perspective}}; and security-preserving optimizations speed up existing defenses~\cite{tran2020clearing,zhao2020invarspec,hajiabadi2024levioso}.
 
 Design-level leaks, some through channels the defenses' papers exclude, include speculative interference in InvisiSpec and Delay-on-Miss, which CleanupSpec does not block~\cite{behnia2021interference}, rollback timing in CleanupSpec~\cite{li2022unxpec}, inclusive caches and store-to-load forwarding in delay and cache-based schemes~\cite{aimoniotis2022doin,chakraborty2022timed}, store channels in STT~\cite{loughlin2021dolma}, timing channels and timers that several defenses do not block~\cite{fustos2020spectrerewind,xiao2023hackyracers}, and reservation-station contention, which STT's and DOLMA's designs do not consider~\cite{cheng2026contention}. Implementation bugs in simulated Spectre defenses include a pending-squash timing leak in STT and SPT, found by Protean's fuzzer~\cite[\S VII-B4b]{protean}, and an SPT bug, found by manual inspection, that untaints transient loads after a pending fault~\cite[\S VII-B4c]{protean}, both since fixed~\cite{nickscommittoSTT,mosier2025sptpr6}. Pensieve's authors found that GhostMinion's gem5 implementation omits leapfrogging, a mechanism the defense relies on~\cite{pensieve}. Kvalsvik shows that ReCon's design leaks when an instruction has several taint sources: STT tracks only the youngest, and once ReCon marks it as already leaked, the instruction executes while an older source is still speculative~\cite[\S 4.3]{kvalsvik2025thesis}. UV3 (\S\ref{sec:bug-otb}) is a different flaw: ReCon's implementation of STT tracks only the oldest source. \toolname{} finds such bugs with one method for every specified defense.
 
